From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: domicile CI <ci@domicile.invalid>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 21:10:42 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] domicile: take DomicileExternalSurface to stable, so no flag
 is needed

run-engine.sh reaches embedExternalSurface() with
--enable-blink-features=DomicileExternalSurface, and switches::kEnableBlinkFeatures
is in bad_flags_prompt.cc's list, so Chromium paints "You are using an
unsupported command-line flag" across the top of every window. The window here
is the desktop, so the banner was the shell's own chrome.

status: "stable" is "Enable this in all Blink configurations". Upstream would
not take a feature to stable on these grounds; this is a fork, and it is the
only configuration that has a browser-side broker for the method to talk to.

This does not remove the banner on its own -- the flag's presence is what
triggers it. It is what lets run-engine.sh drop the flag, which is the other
half and lives outside this package.
---
 .../platform/runtime_enabled_features.json5       | 15 +++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/third_party/blink/renderer/platform/runtime_enabled_features.json5 b/third_party/blink/renderer/platform/runtime_enabled_features.json5
index 8483baa19a3cb..a000b4980b36a 100644
--- a/third_party/blink/renderer/platform/runtime_enabled_features.json5
+++ b/third_party/blink/renderer/platform/runtime_enabled_features.json5
@@ -2469,13 +2469,20 @@
     },
     // Domicile's engine fork: canvas.embedExternalSurface(), which points a
     // <canvas>'s cc::SurfaceLayer at a viz surface the browser brokered to a
-    // compositing producer outside this renderer. Deliberately statusless, so
-    // it is off in every configuration including experimental web platform
-    // features, and on only for a browser started with
-    // --enable-blink-features=DomicileExternalSurface.
+    // compositing producer outside this renderer.
+    //
+    // Stable, so it is on in every configuration of this fork and needs no
+    // command-line flag. That is the whole reason it is stable rather than
+    // statusless: reaching it with --enable-blink-features made Chromium paint
+    // the "You are using an unsupported command-line flag" banner across the
+    // top of the desktop, and the desktop is the whole screen, so the banner
+    // was the shell's own chrome. Upstream would not take a feature to stable
+    // on those grounds; this is a fork, and the only configuration that has a
+    // browser-side broker for the method to talk to.
     // See docs/architecture/ENGINE-FORK.md in the Domicile repository.
     {
       name: "DomicileExternalSurface",
+      status: "stable",
     },
     {
       name: "DocumentPolicyNegotiation",
